<Window xmlns="clr-namespace:MGUI.Core.UI.XAML;assembly=MGUI.Core"
        xmlns:dataBinding="clr-namespace:MGUI.Core.UI.DataBinding;assembly=MGUI.Core"
        Left="0" Top="0" Width="320" Height="240"
        TitleText=""
        IsTitleBarVisible="False"
        IsTopmost="True"
        IsUserResizable="False"
        Padding="0"
        BorderThickness="0"
        Background="rgba(0,0,0,0)">

  <!-- E19.f3b (docs/plan-e19-opcodes.md): the OUI / NON box of the original (slot 3 of its callback table, cfg 0x800A4FEC), versioned as a project asset (parent ADR-0002) next to
       TextBoxScreen.xaml: this file, ChoiceScreen.uiscreen and ChoiceScreen.design.json live in alundra-project/UI/Screens/; the converter catalogues the envelope and never writes
       here. Same shell as TextBoxScreen.xaml: no title bar, a full-screen transparent window whose sole content is a Canvas authored in NATIVE pixels (320x240); the code
       (AlundraChoiceScreen) resizes the window to the real screen bounds and applies the whole integer pixel scale once, as the canvas own RenderTransform.Scale (engine ADR-0006).
       Every value that moves is bound to the window data context, an AlundraChoiceViewModel the presenter writes after each pass of the choice box; what never moves (the rows of
       the frame, of the labels and of the cursor: cfg.y 144, cfg.y + 8, cfg.y - 8) is declared here.

       Draw order (back to front) is the binary's: the frame cells, then the cursor, then the two labels (OT slot 5 then slot 6). No clip: the binary draws the box over the whole
       screen, and the root canvas only cuts what leaves the 320x240 image during the slides. The frame is the baked 128x32 sprite of the choice box
       (g_uiBoxesConfigurationBackgroundMessageChoice, E19.f1), by its fixed id: its name is shared by three assets (png, texture, sprite). The cursor is one of four sprites by name
       (wind_150, wind_173, wind_201, wind_228), as the text box's. The window carries BorderThickness="0" itself (a window draws a border and insets its content otherwise).

       The two labels are font3 texts drawn pixel for pixel (annex view-notes.md section 1.2): Padding="0" (the default 1 pixel padding would shift the glyphs one right and one
       down), LinePadding="0", WrapText="False", inline formatting off (the texts come from the game). The width of a label is its text: the binary draws it as a sprite as wide as the
       text (OUI 19, NON 23). -->

  <Canvas Name="RootCanvas" Width="320" Height="240" Visibility="{dataBinding:MGBinding Path=RootVisibility}">

    <Image Name="Frame" Stretch="None" SourceName="829f31a7-39d1-5fc5-b436-befd59b50985" CanvasLeft="{dataBinding:MGBinding Path=Frame.Left}" CanvasTop="144" />

    <Image Name="Cursor" Stretch="None" SourceName="{dataBinding:MGBinding Path=Cursor.SourceName}" CanvasLeft="{dataBinding:MGBinding Path=Cursor.Left}" CanvasTop="136" />

    <TextBlock Name="Label0" Text="{dataBinding:MGBinding Path=Label0.Text}" CanvasLeft="{dataBinding:MGBinding Path=Label0.Left}" CanvasTop="152" FontFamily="font3" Foreground="White" AllowsInlineFormatting="False" Padding="0" LinePadding="0" WrapText="False" VerticalContentAlignment="Top" />

    <TextBlock Name="Label1" Text="{dataBinding:MGBinding Path=Label1.Text}" CanvasLeft="{dataBinding:MGBinding Path=Label1.Left}" CanvasTop="152" FontFamily="font3" Foreground="White" AllowsInlineFormatting="False" Padding="0" LinePadding="0" WrapText="False" VerticalContentAlignment="Top" />

  </Canvas>
</Window>
